      "writtenOn": "2020-03-20T03:12:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "this seems bad that two err conditions are to be returned, one through an return val, and one through a reference. Is it worth refactoring in a later CL? I understand that we\u0027re trying to differentiate between a read and a fault returned, but it reads differently at first glance. \n\nWhich is why this is a nice catch. \n\nBut then we move on in the logic chain...\n\nAnd then the fault might not be handled which then we get no logging to indicate that it wasn\u0027t handled (and therefore not cleared). Except that...\n\nhandle_fault just prints that we detected one, without doing anything about it, which is a misnomer in it\u0027s own right. This is coding-for-the-future, but it\u0027s not serving a purpose. \n\nthere\u0027s also no terminal else to let us know that we failed to clear the fault if the tcpc_write fails.",
      "revId": "56778557db768b8a408079f4586aadc02e0880f2",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9a2381ee_06d40bcb",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 990,
      "author": {
        "id": 1346479
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-03-20T14:55:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This happens as an interrupt event.  So a different condition, CC_Line_Change, could have given an interrupt and then we get a fault.  In the handling of the first condition we may pick up the fault as well.  When we leave the handler, we could return for the later fault and this would be misleading to anyone looking at the logs.  It would indicate that we caught fault 0x00 and handled it.  Since there is no such thing it looks confusing.  We currently do not use the faults for anything and this is more to clear the FAULT and for debug to be seen in the EC console.  I am not sure a refactor is needed.  If we start using the FAULT for more than this clearing, so the chips will exit problem mode, and debug then I would suggest a rethink on this entire process as well as where in the alert handler it should happen would be in order. A terminal else could be present to indicate there was a problem with I2C getting the information to handle this but not sure it is that important.  Unfortunately a lot of the tcpci code does not bother to even look at errors from i2c, so this would be a big refactor to do it right.",
      "parentUuid": "b7d57a72_cd10cda5",
      "revId": "56778557db768b8a408079f4586aadc02e0880f2",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
